</section>

<section class="svc-detail">
  <div class="container svc-detail-grid">
    <div class="svc-detail-media">
      <img
        class="gw-photo"
        src="/assets/img/services/groundworks-foundations.webp"
        width="800"
        height="400"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        alt="Excavator digging a foundation trench on a groundworks site in Surrey"
      />
    </div>
    <div class="svc-detail-copy">
      <h3 class="section-title">Foundations and build to DPC</h3>
      <p class="lede-dark">Our foundation packages take a plot from excavation through to damp proof course, so the bricklayer or frame contractor inherits an accurate, dimensionally correct substructure. We excavate, place concrete, fix formwork and steel, and build brickwork and blockwork up to DPC on extensions, new-build homes and larger development schemes.</p>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>Strip foundations</li>
        <li>Trench-fill foundations</li>
        <li>Raft foundations</li>
        <li>Block-and-beam floors</li>
        <li>Concrete slabs</li>
        <li>Formwork and steel fixing</li>
        <li>Brickwork and blockwork to DPC</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Foundation type and dimensions are not decided on site. They are governed by the approved drawings, the engineer's specification and the ground conditions found at formation level. Where conditions differ from the design, we stop, record it and refer it back to the engineer and building control.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="svc-detail svc-detail-alt">
  <div class="container svc-detail-grid svc-detail-reverse">
    <div class="svc-detail-media">
      <img
        class="gw-photo"
        src="/assets/img/Surface water drainage.jpg"
        width="768"
        height="576"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        alt="Surface water drainage trench with a perforated land drain bedded in gravel and wrapped in geotextile membrane"
      />
    </div>
    <div class="svc-detail-copy">
      <h3 class="section-title">Drainage and utility trenching</h3>
      <p class="lede-dark">Below-ground infrastructure is where most groundworks programmes are won or lost. We install foul and surface water systems, form soakaways where ground conditions and design allow, build manholes and inspection chambers, and cut and backfill the service trenches and ducting routes the utility providers need.</p>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>Foul and surface water drainage</li>
        <li>Soakaways</li>
        <li>Manholes and inspection chambers</li>
        <li>Water, electricity and gas ducting</li>
        <li>Connections to existing drainage</li>
        <li>Coordination with foundations and levels</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The outcome is a system that falls correctly, connects cleanly into the existing network and still works once the site is finished. That means agreeing invert levels, drainage runs and duct routes against the foundation layout and finished levels before the first trench is opened.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="svc-detail">
  <div class="container svc-detail-grid">
    <div class="svc-detail-media">
      <img
        class="gw-photo"
        src="/assets/img/services/groundworks-siteprep.jpg"
        width="1000"
        height="667"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        alt="Tracked excavator clearing spoil and debris on a wet open site during site preparation"
      />
    </div>
    <div class="svc-detail-copy">
      <h3 class="section-title">Site preparation and excavation</h3>
      <p class="lede-dark">Site preparation turns an awkward plot into somewhere a construction programme can run. We strip and stockpile topsoil, form levels, install temporary access and hardstanding, stabilise poor ground and remove surplus material.</p>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>Topsoil stripping</li>
        <li>Site levelling</li>
        <li>Temporary access</li>
        <li>Ground stabilisation</li>
        <li>Muck-away and disposal</li>
        <li>Material reuse on site</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Plant is matched to the site, not the other way round. Restricted-access excavation behind an occupied house needs small tracked machines and a plan for getting material out; larger commercial excavation needs bigger machines and dumpers on an organised haul route. Reusing suitable material on site cuts lorry movements and disposal cost.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="svc-detail svc-detail-alt">
  <div class="container svc-detail-grid svc-detail-reverse">
    <div class="svc-detail-media">
      <img
        class="gw-photo"
